    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/garcia-sanchez-of-castile"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/garcia-sanchez-of-castile.jpg" alt="García Sánchez of Castile" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/garcia-sanchez-of-castile">García Sánchez of Castile</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Murder_of_Garc%C3%ADa_S%C3%A1nchez_of_Castile_in_1029.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Castile began as a frontier district of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> and León on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Duero</a>, named for the castles that guarded it against Muslim raids. In the tenth century Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernan-gonzalez-of-castile">Fernán González</a> (d. 970) made the county hereditary and largely autonomous. After the murder of the last count, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garcia-sanchez-of-castile">García Sánchez</a>, in 1029, Castile passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III the Great of Navarre</a>, who gave it to his son Ferdinand. In 1037 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-leon">Ferdinand I</a> defeated and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bermudo-iii-of-leon">Bermudo III of León</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tamaron">Battle of Tamarón</a> and was crowned king of León, and he is traditionally counted as the first king of Castile. At his death in 1065 he divided his realms among his sons: Castile went to the eldest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-ii-of-castile">Sancho II</a>, León to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> to García. Sancho II defeated Alfonso at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-llantada">Llantada</a> in 1068 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-golpejera">Golpejera</a> in 1072 and took León, but was murdered in October 1072 while besieging his sister Urraca's city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a>. Alfonso VI then reunited Castile and León, which stayed under one ruler until 1157. In the struggles of these years <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">Rodrigo Díaz de Vivar</a>, El Cid, rose to prominence as Sancho II's commander, before serving Alfonso VI and, in exile, the Muslim rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/burgos"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burgos.jpg" alt="Burgos" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burgos_city_view_facing_south_east.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jardoz, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Castile was a frontier society shaped by war and resettlement (<em>repoblación</em>). The thinly populated lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Duero</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a> were settled by free peasants and townsmen attracted by fueros, local charters such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sepulveda">Sepúlveda</a> (1076) and Cuenca (around 1190), which granted personal freedom, land and self-government. Town councils ruled large rural districts, and their mounted citizen militias, the <em>caballeros villanos</em>, could rise to privileged status by fighting on horseback. Sheep-raising flourished on the empty plains, with flocks moving between summer and winter pastures, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a>, on the pilgrim road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>, grew into a trading town with many French settlers. The conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 1085 brought a large city with Muslim, Jewish and Arabic-speaking Christian (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozarabs">Mozarab</a>) communities under Castilian rule. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kingdoms <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> drew tributes in gold, the <em>parias</em>, which made him one of the richest rulers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Europe</a>, and he pledged an annual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2,000 gold pieces to the abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cluny-abbey">Cluny</a>. Church reform followed: the Roman rite replaced the old Mozarabic liturgy at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-burgos">Council of Burgos</a> in 1080, and French Cluniac monks filled many bishoprics, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-sedirac">Bernard of Sédirac</a>, archbishop of Toledo from 1086.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sagrajas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-sagrajas.jpg" alt="Battle of Sagrajas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sagrajas">Battle of Sagrajas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalha_de_Zalaka_-_Hist%C3%B3ria_de_Portugal,_popular_e_ilustrada.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Roque Gameiro, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom's armies combined royal and noble cavalry with the militias of the frontier towns, which defended and often extended the border on their own initiative. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> exploited the divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and on 25 May 1085 occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, the old Visigothic capital, after years of pressure. The fall of Toledo prompted the taifa rulers to call in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, who defeated Alfonso at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sagrajas">Battle of Sagrajas</a> in 1086 and again at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ucles-1108">Battle of Uclés</a> in 1108, where his only son Sancho was killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> in 1094 and held it until his death in 1099, but it was abandoned in 1102. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII</a> recovered the offensive, campaigned into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> in 1147 with Genoese and Catalan help, though it was lost in 1157. To defend the exposed frontier the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-calatrava">Order of Calatrava</a> was founded in 1158, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-santiago">Order of Santiago</a>, founded in León in 1170, soon settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ucles">Uclés</a>; these military orders held castles and large estates across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mancha">La Mancha</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> took Cuenca in 1177 but was crushed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alarcos">Battle of Alarcos</a> in 1195. With papal backing he gathered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-ii-of-aragon">Peter II of Aragon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-vii-of-navarre">Sancho VII of Navarre</a> and crusaders from beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, and on 16 July 1212 destroyed the Almohad army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile.jpg" alt="Alfonso VI of Castile" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI of Castile</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlfonsoVI_of_Castile.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Uploaded by MaiDireLollo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Royal government was personal and itinerant. Kings travelled between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a>, León, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and other cities with a household of officials, a chancery of clerics and a court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, the <em>curia regia</em>, which advised on war, law and succession. Counts and great nobles, such as the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-lara">Lara</a> and Castro families, held lands and offices from the king, and their quarrels dominated royal minorities, above all that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> after 1158. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII</a> used the imperial title, <em>imperator totius Hispaniae</em>, to claim leadership over the other Christian rulers, and in 1135 Alfonso VII was crowned emperor in León, receiving homage from the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, the count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and Muslim princes. The towns were governed by their councils under their fueros, and in the late twelfth century their representatives began to attend royal assemblies, the roots of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-castile">Cortes of Castile</a>. Succession could pass through women: Urraca ruled as queen from 1109 to 1126, though her marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I of Aragon</a> ended in war, and in 1217 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengaria-of-castile">Berenguela</a>, Alfonso VIII's daughter, inherited the throne and handed it at once to her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III</a>. Alfonso VIII also founded the first university of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palencia">Palencia</a>, around 1208–1212.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/almagest"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/almagest.jpg" alt="Almagest" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/almagest">Almagest</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Claudius_Ptolemaeus,_Almagestum,_1515.djvu" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ptolemy, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom made Castile the leading power of Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and pushed the frontier from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Duero</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-morena">Sierra Morena</a>. The conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> was a turning point in relations between Christian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Muslim Spain</a>: it was the first great city of al-Andalus to fall, and in Toledo, where Archbishop Raymond (1125–1152) encouraged the work, scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerard-of-cremona">Gerard of Cremona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominicus-gundissalinus">Dominicus Gundissalinus</a> translated Arabic works of philosophy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a> and medicine into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, including Ptolemy's <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almagest">Almagest</a></em> and works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a> and al-Farabi, transmitting them to the schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The victory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Las Navas de Tolosa</a> broke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> power and opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a> to conquest; within a generation <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> (1236) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> (1248). The career of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a> inspired the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantar-de-mio-cid">Cantar de mio Cid</a></em>, probably composed around 1200, the earliest surviving epic in Castilian, a language that would later spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Castile's fueros, town councils and military orders shaped the society of the reconquered south, and its sheep-raising economy prepared the later wool trade. The kingdom's claims to lead the reconquest and to inherit Visigothic Spain became central to the ideology of the later Spanish monarchy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile.jpg" alt="Alfonso VII of León and Castile" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII of León and Castile</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfonso-Privilegium.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonymous; derivative work by Agricolae, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Castile did not fall; it was absorbed into a larger union. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII</a> died in 1157 he divided his realms again, leaving Castile to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-castile">Sancho III</a> and León to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II</a>. Sancho died in 1158, and the long minority of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> brought noble civil war and Leonese and Navarrese encroachment, while for the next seventy years the two kingdoms often fought each other over their border and their shares of the frontier with the Muslims. Alfonso VIII's death in 1214 was followed by another minority, that of Henry I, who was killed by a falling roof tile in 1217. His sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengaria-of-castile">Berenguela</a>, who had been married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon">Alfonso IX of León</a>, passed the Castilian crown to their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III</a>. When Alfonso IX died in 1230, he tried to leave León to his daughters from his first marriage, but by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-benavente">agreement of Benavente</a>, negotiated by Berenguela and the daughters' mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teresa-of-portugal-queen-of-leon">Teresa of Portugal</a>, the daughters renounced their rights in exchange for pensions, and Ferdinand III became king of León. The union of 1230 proved permanent. The combined realm, known as the Crown of Castile, conquered most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> within a generation and later, with the Crown of Aragon, formed the Spanish monarchy.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Castile turned a frontier county into the leading power of Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 1085, breaking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Las Navas de Tolosa</a> in 1212 and, through its permanent union with León in 1230, laying the foundation of the Crown of Castile and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</em></p>

